  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to utilize a hierarchical optimization framework that combines local gradient descent with global exploration phases. Unlike the parent's binary swapping approach, this method:\n\n- Uses a continuous relaxation initially allows for faster convergence and escapes discrete plateaus\n- Maintains multi-scale representations (coarse-grained and fine-grained) to balance exploration and exploitation\n- Adapts the optimization trajectory based on the current solution quality and dimensionality\n\nThe bottleneck addressed is the limited effective search space of binary swapping which often traps in local optima. Here's how this approach helps:\n\n- Gradient-based refinement phase escapes plateaus by following steepest descent\n- Overlapped correlation analysis identifies promising directions faster\n- Warm restarts from quantized solutions prevents premature convergence\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation:\n- Primary: Continuous real-valued h(x) \u2208 [0,1]n with \u2211h = n/2\n- Secondary: Recurrently quantized to binary representation when mature\n\nObjective:\nMinimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)[1-h(x+k)]dx\nwith continuous relaxation min\u209c max\u2096 \u222bh\u209c(x)[1-h\u209c(x+k)]dx\n\nConstraints:\n0 \u2264 h\u209c(i) \u2264 1, \u2211h\u209c(i) \u2248 n/2\n\nConvex reformulation:\n1. Overlap correlation component: \n   R(k,t) = \u2211\u2096\u207f h\u209c(i)\u00b7(1-h\u209c(i+k))\u00b7dx\n   Objective: min\u209c max\u2096 R(k,t)\u00b7dx\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStep 1: Setup and maternal candidate\n- If parent (n=250) exists, load it and minimize C\u2085 for it\n- Else create a Thue-Morse based binary initialization (30%, n/n/2 ones)\n- Score across majority/minority directions\n\nStep 2: Gradient descent with hybrid_regularizer\n```python\ndef hybrid_step(x, mu=0.8, sigma=0.2):\n    # Introduce continuous-like perturbation\n    perturb = np.random.normal(0, sigma, len(x))\n    candidate = mu*x + (1-mu)*perturb\n    # Project to [0,1]\n    inc = (candidate < 1)*candidate + (candidate <0)*np.clip(candidate, -0.1,1)\n    candidate_true = np.clip(project_feasible(inc),0,1)\n    return candidate_true\n```\n\nStep 3: Multi-stage gradient descent\n1. Global expansion: Strided~1.0 cooling schedule\n2. Intermediate descent: \u03bc=0.95, \u03c3=0.02\n3. Final polishing: Curvilinear descent with \u03c3 adaptive to curvature\n\nStep 4: Quantization and repair\n```python\ndef quantize_solution(h_truth, h_sample, delta=0.05):\n    # Project continuous to binary representation\n    thresholds = np.linspace(delta, 1-delta, len(h_truth)-1)\n    rounded = np.digitize(h_truth, thresholds)-1\n    #Apply repair to maintain sum\n    return project_feasible(rounded)\n```\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n```python\nBASE_CONFIG = {\n    'T_init': 0.9,\n    'alpha_slope': 0.8,\n    'n_refine': 2000,\n    'sigma_ratio': 0.05,\n    'reinitialize_pct': 0.05,\n    'multiplier': 1.2\n}\n```\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- Initial global sampling: 20%\n- Local trust region: 50%\n- Final polishing: 30%\n\nFallback strategy:\n1. If exceed time budget: persist best in top~20% or restart from warmed-up value\n2. If high curvature detected: increase curvature threshold\n3. If objective ~ 0.3808: relax to 0.38 for termination\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final repair: Ensure sorted by permutation\n- Evaluate final solution with double-check: np.isclose(sum(final_h)/n, 0.5)\n- Termination condition: consecutive 100 steps \u0394C\u2085_min < 0.00001\n- Return format: (final_h, final_C\u2085, n=250)",
